# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purpose:
#   Excludes paths from the REPOSITORY-ROOT build context. Exactly one image is
#   built from that context: services/batch-service/Dockerfile, whose own header
#   records why -- it compiles the unpublished common-lib sibling from source and
#   the inherited Checkstyle gate reads config/checkstyle, both of which sit
#   outside its own tree, so no narrower context can build it. Without this file
#   the daemon receives the whole working tree, which is neither small nor safe.
#
#   The repository's other image, data-migration/Dockerfile, is built from
#   data-migration/ as its context and is therefore governed by
#   data-migration/.dockerignore instead. The daemon resolves this file from the
#   context root, so this one has no effect on that build and that one has no
#   effect on this build. Each context needs its own file; neither is redundant.
#
# Parameters (the inputs this file governs):
#   One input: the set of paths the daemon receives when a build is started from
#   the repository root. Every pattern below is matched against paths relative
#   to that root. A pattern removes a path from the context entirely, so a
#   removed path cannot be reached by any COPY instruction and cannot appear in
#   any layer.
#
# Return values:
#   None directly. Its effect is observable as the "transferring context" size a
#   build reports and as the absence of the excluded paths from every layer.
#
# Exceptions or errors:
#   A pattern that excludes something a Dockerfile copies makes the build fail
#   on a missing path, which is loud and immediate. The reverse mistake -- a
#   pattern too narrow -- fails silently and is what the rationale below is
#   written to prevent, so a new exclusion is safer to add than an existing one
#   is to remove.
#
# Governing convention:
#   docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md
#
# WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
#   1. Refactoring Rationale: this file exists at the ROOT rather than beside the
#      Dockerfile it governs, because the daemon resolves .dockerignore from the
#      build CONTEXT and not from the location of the Dockerfile. The batch image
#      is built from the root with an explicit --file, so a copy of these rules
#      placed in services/batch-service/ would be ignored completely -- present,
#      plausible, and doing nothing. This placement is the only one that takes
#      effect for that build, and it is the reason the second image needs its own
#      file at its own context root rather than inheriting these rules.
#   2. Assumptions: the three reasons for each exclusion below are secret
#      exposure, layer contamination and context size, in that order of
#      importance. Secret exposure is the reason .git and .env* are named first:
#      a build that receives .git receives every value ever committed and later
#      removed, and a COPY of a directory silently includes any local
#      environment file inside it. Layer contamination is the reason build
#      output is excluded: a host-built target/ or node_modules/ copied into a
#      builder can shadow what the container itself compiles, producing an image
#      whose contents depend on the developer's machine. Context size is real
#      but last: it makes builds slow, not wrong.
#   3. Alternatives Considered: naming only the paths the two Dockerfiles copy
#      and excluding everything else with a leading `*` plus re-inclusions. That
#      is the stricter shape and it was rejected on maintenance grounds -- every
#      new COPY would need a matching re-inclusion, and the failure mode of
#      forgetting one is a build error whose cause is in this file rather than in
#      the Dockerfile being edited. The enumerated form fails in the safer
#      direction for this repository, where the reference-only baseline trees are
#      never copied by any image and cannot appear by accident.
#   4. Trade-offs: the reference-only baseline trees (app/, tests/, scripts/,
#      samples/) are deliberately NOT excluded, even though no image copies
#      them. Excluding them would shrink the context, and the reason not to is
#      that the Dockerfile cites baseline paths in its own comments as the
#      specification for what it builds; a reader who runs a build from a context
#      missing those trees cannot check a citation from inside it. The cost is a
#      larger context transfer and nothing else, because a path no COPY names
#      never enters a layer.
# =============================================================================

# WHY : Assumptions: version-control metadata is the highest-value exclusion in
#       this file. It carries the full history of every tracked file, so any
#       value that was ever committed and later removed is still retrievable from
#       it -- which makes a context containing .git a durable copy of every past
#       secret, regardless of what the current tree looks like. Nothing in either
#       image needs it: neither Dockerfile stamps a commit identity, and if one
#       ever should, the identity belongs in a build argument rather than in a
#       copy of the repository's history.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# WHY : Assumptions: local environment files are excluded by pattern rather than
#       by name, because the risk is the file nobody remembered. .env.example is
#       committed and carries variable NAMES with no values by design, while a
#       .env, .env.local or .env.prod is a developer's own file with real values
#       in it, is untracked, and is exactly what a broad COPY would pick up. The
#       pattern covers the whole family so a new suffix does not need a new rule.
.env
.env.*

# WHY : Refactoring Rationale: build output is excluded so that what an image
#       contains is what the image built. The batch image compiles the
#       unpublished common-lib sibling from source inside the builder; if a
#       host-built services/*/target/ were transferred, a stale jar could be
#       present at a path the build expects to create, and the resulting image
#       would depend on the state of the machine that ran the build rather than
#       on the commit. That failure is invisible on the machine that produced it
#       and reproducible nowhere else.
target
**/target
build
**/build

# WHY : Assumptions: the same reasoning covers the front-end trees, plus one
#       specific hazard of their own. A node_modules/ built on the host may hold
#       native modules compiled for the host's own libc and architecture, so
#       copying it into a Linux image can install binaries that cannot run there
#       -- a failure that appears at runtime rather than at build time. The
#       front-end image installs its dependencies inside the builder from the
#       committed lock file, which is what makes them reproducible.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist

# WHY : Assumptions: infrastructure working directories and STATE are excluded,
#       and state is the reason this rule is not merely tidiness. A Terraform
#       state file records resource attributes as applied, which for this
#       architecture includes generated database credentials and secret ARNs, so
#       a state file inside a build context is a secret inside a build context.
#       The .terraform/ directory additionally holds provider binaries that are
#       hundreds of megabytes and of no use to any image here.
.terraform
**/.terraform
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan

# WHY : Assumptions: interpreter and tool caches are excluded because they are
#       machine-local and can shadow what the image installs. A .venv/ built on
#       the host pins absolute paths from that host into its scripts, so a copy
#       of it inside an image is broken in a way that is confusing to diagnose;
#       __pycache__ directories hold bytecode compiled by whichever interpreter
#       version the host happened to run.
.venv
**/.venv
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc
.pytest_cache
**/.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
**/.ruff_cache
.mypy_cache
**/.mypy_cache

# WHY : Trade-offs: the temporary validation artifacts an agent or a developer
#       may leave in the working tree are excluded by their agreed prefix. They
#       are never committed, so this rule only matters for a build run from a
#       dirty tree -- which is precisely the situation in which an unexpected
#       file reaching a layer would be hardest to notice.
blitzy_adhoc_test_*
**/blitzy_adhoc_test_*

# WHY : Assumptions: editor and operating-system metadata is excluded last
#       because it is the least consequential of these categories, not because
#       it is harmless: an editor directory can carry local tool settings and
#       occasionally credentials for a language server or a database client.
.idea
.vscode
*.swp
.DS_Store
